.TH cut 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
cut \- select columns or fields from each line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cut
.BI \-c list
[\fIfile\fR ...]
.br
.B cut
.BI \-f list
[\fB\-d\fI x\fR] [\fB\-s\fR] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cut
writes selected parts of each input line to standard output.  With no
.IR file ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
A
.I list
is a comma separated set of numbers and ranges counting from 1:
.BR 1,4,7 ,
.BR 3\-5 ,
.B 3\-
for everything from field 3 on, or
.B \-5
for everything up to field 5.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c list
Select by character position.
.TP
.BI \-b list
The same thing: on FUZIX a character is a byte.
.TP
.BI \-f list
Select by field.
.TP
.BI \-d x
Use
.I x
as the field separator instead of tab.
.TP
.B \-s
With
.BR \-f ,
skip lines that contain no separator.  Without it, such lines are passed
through whole.
.TP
.B \-n
Accepted for POSIX and ignored.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd
# cut -c1-8 file
# ls -l | cut -c40-
.fi
.SH BUGS
.B \-i
is meant to make a run of separators count as one, which matters for
columns lined up with spaces.  The test that guards it is inverted, so
the flag is cleared in exactly the case where it would have worked:
.B \-i
does nothing in this build, and using it with
.B \-f
also prints a warning naming
.B \-s
rather than
.BR \-i .
.PP
There is no
.BR \-\-complement ,
and
.B \-d
takes only a single character.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR awk (1),
.BR sed (1),
.BR sort (1)
